{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2233 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "efbce831dff07d35af023018f6529e948c88e123f7b3fbf6e83d158555917af4" :edb/db-before-t 2232 :edb/db-after-t 2233 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535545 50 #inst "2026-10-09T02:14:10.813Z" 13194139535545 true] [17592186092040 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/0bb9e5e7315d4bcd5fc21700fdd2597f6d9b585a109145de53c6f94e9530eabf.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ (shown in blue in the picture above)." 13194139535545 true] [17592186092040 1100 "Find the arc length of the polar curve $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$ (shown in blue in the picture above).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/999/Source/Images/7b8c89d9e599587ac6274b08d6202514.png)" 13194139535545 false] [17592186092040 1145 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1-\\cos\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now use the half-angle formula $1-\\cos\\theta =2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}\\right)$ to give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={-4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535545 true] [17592186092040 1145 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr=2(1-\\cos\\theta)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(2 - 2\\cos θ)^{2} + (2\\sin θ)^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{4 - 8\\cos θ + 4\\cos^{2} θ + 4\\sin^{2} θ}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{4 - 8\\cos θ + 4}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{8 - 8\\cos θ}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{1 - \\cos θ}dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now use the half-angle formula\n\n$$\n1-\\cos\\theta =2\\sin^2\\left(\\dfrac{\\theta}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nto give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= 2\\sqrt{2}∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{2\\sin^{2} (\\frac{θ}{2})}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot ∫_{0}^{π}\\sin (\\frac{θ}{2})dθ \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot 2(\\cos (\\frac{θ}{2})) \\mid _{0}^{π} \\\\\n&=-8(\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) - \\cos 0) \\\\\n&=-8(0 - 1) \\\\\n&= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535545 false] [17592186092070 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/e5647c0f19727d1e626e637464cc57444e0bc0f2f8e99a4afd15d3c3631dc3f0.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the arc length of the polar curve $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi,$ shown in blue in the diagram above." 13194139535545 true] [17592186092070 1100 "Find an integral expression for the arc length of the polar curve $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi$, shown in blue in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/999/Source/Images/9a98033ef7124397b87e336d2d7778e9.png)" 13194139535545 false] [17592186092070 1145 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=3-\\sin(7\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} =-7\\cos(7\\theta)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta ))^{2}+(-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+49{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+{sin}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+1+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7\\theta )+48{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535545 true] [17592186092070 1145 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta)$ is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nr=3-\\sin(7\\theta)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} =-7\\cos(7\\theta)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL &= ∫_{θ_{1}}^{θ_{2}}\\sqrt{r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(3 - \\sin (7θ))^{2} + (-7\\cos (7θ))^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{9 - 6\\sin (7θ) + \\sin^{2} (7θ) + 49\\cos^{2} (7θ)}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{9 - 6\\sin (7θ) + \\sin^{2} (7θ) + \\cos^{2} (7θ) + 48\\cos^{2} (7θ)}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{9 - 6\\sin (7θ) + 1 + 48\\cos^{2} (7θ)}dθ \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{10 - 6\\sin (7θ) + 48\\cos^{2} (7θ)}dθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535545 false] [17592186092320 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354550 13194139535545 true] [17592186092320 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354571 13194139535545 true] [17592186092320 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354578 13194139535545 true] [17592186092320 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354585 13194139535545 true] [17592186092320 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354592 13194139535545 true] [17592186092321 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354557 13194139535545 true] [17592186092321 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354564 13194139535545 true] [17592186092321 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354599 13194139535545 true] [17592186092321 1020 #edb/ref 17592186354606 13194139535545 true] [17592186092869 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535545 true] [17592186092869 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/085be1e624ae99afee256d2a9591f17d2f33f688364b6e59fdcd7c415206bda7.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." 13194139535545 true] [17592186092869 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/999/Source/Images/q-9532.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r = 2\\sin θ$ for $\\frac{π}{2} \\le θ \\le \\frac{5π}{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)." 13194139535545 false] [17592186092869 1145 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}1\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6} \\\\  & =2(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354550 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354551 13194139535545 true] [17592186354550 1089 "q-103831" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354550 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535545 true] [17592186354550 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/b1a0f5b8438b5abeafafccadd5a3a33572441c4d58d2e97f7254bd28987c223c.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the arc length of the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ (shown in blue in the diagram above)?" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354550 1113 #uuid "fff3852c-f58f-5d0e-926d-65e98b09f1bd" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354550 1145 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}+(-12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta ))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+144{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16({cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta ))}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354552 13194139535545 true] [17592186354551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354553 13194139535545 true] [17592186354551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354554 13194139535545 true] [17592186354551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354555 13194139535545 true] [17592186354551 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354556 13194139535545 true] [17592186354551 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535545 true] [17592186354551 1041 "selection" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354551 1104 #edb/ref 17592186354555 13194139535545 true] [17592186354551 1110 #uuid "b08bd99f-b030-50dd-9c47-56fe59ea99ec" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354552 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535545 true] [17592186354552 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535545 true] [17592186354552 1111 #uuid "dc7a8197-517f-5255-b4cc-b24ac7d88037" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354553 1034 "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535545 true] [17592186354553 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535545 true] [17592186354553 1111 #uuid "569d515c-995c-55ce-84b6-65ff451afaf4" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354554 1034 "\\frac{4}{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535545 true] [17592186354554 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535545 true] [17592186354554 1111 #uuid "3fa00487-577c-5603-981f-ce3ce816f029" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354555 1034 "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )+9{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535545 true] [17592186354555 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535545 true] [17592186354555 1111 #uuid "f6ce3b45-73fb-5085-8a2f-6e3eaf703126" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354556 1034 "3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{5\\pi /6}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )-3{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " 13194139535545 true] [17592186354556 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535545 true] [17592186354556 1111 #uuid "96c51cac-1f0f-5a9e-8ffd-7e46012efaba" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354557 1017 #edb/ref 17592186354558 13194139535545 true] [17592186354557 1089 "q-103837" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354557 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535545 true] [17592186354557 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067289/assets/3387790df770ac8645058c6f460b1f1285629d18d765668e362a8070ae4035ed.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi $ (shown in blue in the diagram above).\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the following identity: $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}.$*" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354557 1113 #uuid "a9fbe6a6-9f8e-565c-aaf7-05ef27741553" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354557 1145 "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the identity for $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\sqrt{\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using $x=\\theta $ in the previous identity, the integral can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={4\\cdot 2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354559 13194139535545 true] [17592186354558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354560 13194139535545 true] [17592186354558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354561 13194139535545 true] [17592186354558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354562 13194139535545 true] [17592186354558 1000 #edb/ref 17592186354563 13194139535545 true] [17592186354558 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535545 true] [17592186354558 1041 "selection" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354558 1104 #edb/ref 17592186354560 13194139535545 true] [17592186354558 1110 #uuid "9da43ea2-e27e-53f4-b67c-512326b3df0f" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354559 1034 "\\sqrt{2}" 13194139535545 true] [17592186354559 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535545 true] [17592186354559 1111 #uuid "6f7aaa83-03b2-50b0-85ad-04f7fedce162" 13194139535